---
term: Devolution
section: life-cycle
order: 35
definition: The end of the formation gradient at which a [[parent corporation]] organizes a subsidiary charter to establish itself on a jurisdiction, and then grants it rights according to their mutual articles.
example: A national health corporation devolves part of its mandate to a new regional pandemic-preparedness corporation. The act of devolution sets the new corporation's budget, gives it jurisdiction over medical stockpiles, and schedules a review in five years.
seeAlso:
  - charter-formation
  - voluntary-admission
  - contract
---
Devolution suits cases where a parent needs a dedicated body to pursue part of its own objective. The subsidiary holds a [[contract]] with the parent from its devolution until [[admission]]. The opposite end is [[voluntary admission]].

The name comes from constitutional practice, where a central authority creates a subnational body by statute and grants it powers. The Scotland Act 1998, which created the Scottish Parliament, is an example. Alfred Stepan calls a federation that forms this way *holding-together*, as opposed to a *coming-together* federation that forms by [[conversion]].
